1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,163
Now the other way to bring a model: a cloud provider with your own API key.

2
00:00:03,163 --> 00:00:07,000
It's a couple of fields — and I want to be very clear about exactly where that key ends up.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,917
Open Settings, Providers, and click Add provider.

4
00:00:09,917 --> 00:00:15,214
There are three fields: a name you choose, the base URL of the service, and your API key.

5
00:00:15,214 --> 00:00:17,000
Let's fill them in for OpenAI.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,615
Most providers speak the same OpenAI-compatible format, so it's just a different base URL each time.

7
00:00:21,615 --> 00:00:24,985
Here are the common ones — OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter, Groq, Together.

8
00:00:24,985 --> 00:00:29,000
Groq and OpenRouter even have real free tiers, so you can try a cloud model at no cost.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,191
Here's the important part.

10
00:00:30,191 --> 00:00:34,725
When you save, your key is written to your operating system's credential store — not into settings.

11
00:00:34,725 --> 00:00:35,321
json on disk.

12
00:00:35,321 --> 00:00:37,977
And it's only ever sent to the one provider it belongs to.

13
00:00:37,977 --> 00:00:41,000
It never passes through us, and it never touches your transcripts.

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,125
One small thing you might notice.

15
00:00:42,125 --> 00:00:46,932
Anthropic is listed on its own because it's a native integration — it uses Anthropic's messages API rather than the OpenAI-compatible format.

16
00:00:46,932 --> 00:00:50,000
You don't need to do anything different; just pick Anthropic as the kind and it's handled.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,873
Local or cloud, you've now got a model connected.

18
00:00:51,873 --> 00:00:55,465
In the next episode we close the loop — sending your very first message and watching it reply.

19
00:00:55,465 --> 00:00:56,000
See you there.

